=== p2-windows - W7 - page:system/Chatbot (WinUI 3 / Fluent) ===
Parity unit : page:system/Chatbot   kind=page   web route=/chatbot
Web source  : web/src/features/system/pages/ChatbotPage.tsx
ADR refs    : ADR-002, ADR-004, ADR-005, ADR-006, ADR-011, ADR-013, ADR-014, ADR-015
Runner      : .NET 10.0.300 SDK present (dotnet --version 10.0.300) - NOT blocked.

=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), <Name>.Source.cs (the generated-client feed), <Name>.ViewModel.cs
  (INotifyPropertyChanged state holder) and <Name>.cs (the Fluent view), exactly as the sibling W7
  HelpPage / QuickStatsPage / AlertRulesPage. The generated prompt's idealized
  "TeslaSync/Features/System/ChatbotPage.xaml + ChatbotPageViewModel.cs" is mapped onto these real conventions;
  "ObservableObject ViewModel" is satisfied by the established INotifyPropertyChanged state holder every sibling
  page uses. The namespace is TeslaSync.App.FeatureViews.SystemOps (NOT ...System - a `.System` sub-namespace
  shadows the global System namespace; SystemOps also aligns with RouteGroup.SystemOps, the manifest's system/
  group, matching HelpPage).
- Navigation entry: RouteTable.cs ALREADY maps Page("Chatbot","chatbot",RouteGroup.SystemOps,"Chatbot","\uE8BD")
  (the web /chatbot route, shown in nav). The only shell wiring needed was the page-factory registration in
  ShellWindow.xaml.cs: PageFactory.Register(ChatbotRegistration.RouteName, () => new ChatbotPage()). No
  RouteTable edit was required.
- Data binding (ADR-004): the manifest declares FIVE data sources - useChatSessions, useChatHistory,
  useSendChatMessage, useRenameChatSession, useDeleteChatSession. They are bound through one IChatbotFeed port
  (ChatbotPage.Source.cs): FetchSessionsAsync (GET /chatbot/sessions), FetchHistoryAsync (GET
  /chatbot/history?session_id=), SendMessageAsync (POST /chatbot/), RenameSessionAsync (PATCH
  /chatbot/sessions/{id}) and DeleteSessionAsync (DELETE /chatbot/sessions/{id}). The default EmptyChatbotFeed
  resolves to the empty welcome (the shell ctor); the generated-client-backed ChatbotClientFeed binds the five
  generated operation ids (post_api_v1_chatbot, get_api_v1_chatbot_history, get_api_v1_chatbot_sessions,
  patch_api_v1_chatbot_sessions_id, delete_api_v1_chatbot_sessions_id) - all asserted to resolve against the
  generated TeslaSync.Windows.Generated.Api.ApiEndpoints table by the test
  Every_chatbot_operation_resolves_against_the_generated_table. The history GET's session_id query param appends
  cleanly because the descriptor declares no query params (GeneratedApiClient.BuildQuery passes it through). The
  ChatbotPageViewModel (P1/S8 seam) owns the sessions, the active conversation, the composer draft, the sidebar
  visibility and the conversation state; the view never performs HTTP. The shared ChatMessageItem / SuggestedPrompts
  / AIThinkingDots W3 surfaces are reused, not re-cloned.
- Three data states (manifest: loading / empty / success) + a defensive Error branch (ADR-011): the conversation
  panel (GlassPanel1) renders loading (a TsSkeleton bubble column while the sessions/history read is in flight),
  empty (web messages.length === 0 -> the Helix mark + "How can Helix help you?" + "Ask about your vehicles,
  drives, charging, and more" + the SuggestedPrompts chip strip), success (the message list, each row a shared
  ChatMessageItem) and the honest Error surface (a TsQueryError + Retry, never a blank box). The composer and the
  conversation panel are ALWAYS visible.
- Panels (ADR-005): GlassPanel1 = the conversation TsGlassPanel (Mica/glass via the W1 surface tokens) hosting the
  loading/empty/success/error body plus the composer (web ChatbotPage.tsx main `<GlassPanel className="flex
  flex-col flex-1 ...">`); GlassPanel2 = the thinking-indicator TsGlassPanel hosting the in-button AIThinkingDots
  ("Helix is thinking..."), shown while IsSending (web ChatbotPage.tsx `<GlassPanel className="!p-3 ...">`). No
  hardcoded colours/typography - DisplayTokens (TsColor*/TsRadius* + the cyan accent token) drive light/dark/
  high-contrast from W1.
- Charts/maps: none declared (none built). Live/SSE: the five declared data sources are REST reads/writes, none
  is a live stream, so no >2-min staleness pill is owed (ADR-013 N/A; the AI-on SSE path useAiStream is a
  separate withAiFeature surface, not one of this page's manifest data sources).
- i18n (ADR-014): every visible literal resolves through the ILocalizer facade with the web key names + English
  defaults. The thirteen PARITY_REQUIRED keys (chatbot.title/subtitle/history/aria.conversation/inputLabel/
  placeholder/actions.send/actions.stop/actions.stopHint/actions.stopStreaming/howCanIHelp/askAbout/thinking) are
  already present verbatim in Strings/en/Resources.resw as translation.chatbot.* with the web English values (added
  with the W3 chatbot components); no catalog edit was needed. The sidebar copy (newChat/noSessions/session.*/
  aria.renameSession/aria.deleteSession/delete.*) is likewise routed through the localizer. The one irreducible
  literal "chatbot.placeholder" (the web i18n key name) carries a `// parity:allow` marker in the Model + test
  exactly as TsTextarea.cs does for the WinUI hint API - it is the web key, not a stub.
- a11y (ADR-015): the conversation host is a Polite live region named chatbot.aria.conversation; the History
  sidebar is a Navigation landmark; the composer, Send/Stop and every session row carry Narrator names; the Helix
  mark / decorative icons are AccessibilityView.Raw; Enter sends / Shift+Enter inserts a newline (web keyboard
  contract); the page exposes a Group automation peer; DisplayTokens drive light/dark + high-contrast.
- Units: no unit-bearing fields on this surface (pure conversation text) - SI converters N/A.

